        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The word:</strong> monster comes from the Latin <em>monstrum</em>, an omen or warning, from <em>monere</em>, \"to warn\". Many folklore monsters were exactly that: a warning to keep children safe.</li><li><strong>Under the bed:</strong> the bogeyman has no single shape. He's whatever lurks in the dark corner, which is why under-the-bed names are all fingers, whispers and waiting.</li><li><strong>In the swamp:</strong> English folklore has Jenny Greenteeth, a river hag said to drag children into weedy ponds. The story kept children away from deep water.</li><li><strong>In the sea:</strong> the kraken comes from Norwegian sailors' tales, and was described by the Danish-Norwegian bishop Erik Pontoppidan in his natural history of Norway in the 1750s.</li><li><strong>In the mountains:</strong> Scandinavian trolls live under the hills and, in many tales, turn to stone in sunlight.</li><li><strong>Eldritch:</strong> an old Scots word for weird or unearthly, made famous by H. P. Lovecraft's tales of things too strange to describe.</li></ul>"
